    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "From the standpoint of the estimation of individual effects, the Cockerham-Weir model has some limitations under this design. $ n_{i} $ and $ m_{i} $, and $ n_{i} $ and $ p_{i} $ are confounded, so only $ (2n_{i} + m_{i} + p_{i}) $ and $ (m_{i} - p_{i}) $ are estimable. An alternative approach is the reciprocal-effects model of Griffing (1956),",
